    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following paranasal sinuses is the most common site of origin for sinonasal malignancies?', '["Maxillary sinus", "Ethmoid sinus", "Sphenoid sinus", "Frontal sinus", "None, they occur equally"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The maxillary sinus is the most frequent site of sinonasal malignancies, accounting for approximately 60% of cases, followed by the nasal cavity and the ethmoid sinuses.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 60-year-old man presents with unilateral epiphora (excessive tearing), double vision (diplopia), and a bulging eye (proptosis). A mass is found in the maxillary sinus. Which wall of the maxillary sinus has the tumor likely invaded?', '["Medial wall", "Anterior wall", "Posterior wall", "Superior wall (roof)", "Inferior wall (floor)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The roof of the maxillary sinus is the floor of the orbit. Tumor invasion through this bony partition affects the orbital contents, extraocular muscles, and the nasolacrimal duct, causing epiphora and diplopia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Tumor invasion through the posterior wall of the maxillary sinus places which critical anatomical space at immediate risk?', '["Anterior cranial fossa", "Pterygopalatine fossa", "Oral cavity", "Nasopharynx", "Orbital apex"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The posterior wall of the maxillary sinus borders the pterygopalatine fossa, which contains the maxillary artery, the maxillary nerve (V2), and the pterygopalatine ganglion. Invasion here allows perineural spread toward the middle cranial fossa.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is Ohngren''s line, and what is its significance in maxillary sinus cancer?', '["A line connecting the medial canthus of the eye to the angle of the mandible; tumors superior/posterior to this line have a worse prognosis", "A vertical line down the middle of the face", "A horizontal line connecting the pupils", "A line separating the hard and soft palate", "A surgical incision line for maxillectomy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ohngren''s line is an imaginary line from the medial canthus to the angle of the mandible. Tumors of the "suprastructure" (above/behind the line) invade the orbit, cribriform plate, and pterygopalatine fossa earlier and carry a worse prognosis than "infrastructure" tumors.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which histologic subtype accounts for the vast majority (roughly 80%) of malignancies arising in the maxillary sinus?', '["Adenocarcinoma", "Esthesioneuroblastoma", "Squamous cell carcinoma", "Mucosal melanoma", "Sinonasal undifferentiated carcinoma (SNUC)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'As with most of the upper aerodigestive tract, squamous cell carcinoma is the most common histological type of malignancy in the maxillary sinus.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with chronic exposure to nickel dust or leather tanning chemicals is at an increased occupational risk for which sinonasal malignancy?', '["Adenoid cystic carcinoma", "Squamous cell carcinoma", "Osteosarcoma", "Lymphoma", "Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'While hardwood dust is classically linked to adenocarcinoma, occupational exposure to nickel refining, chromium, and leather tanning is strongly associated with an increased risk of sinonasal squamous cell carcinoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 65-year-old male is diagnosed with Sinonasal Undifferentiated Carcinoma (SNUC). What characterizes the typical clinical behavior of this specific tumor?', '["It is a slow-growing, indolent, low-grade tumor", "It primarily affects the lower lip", "It is extremely aggressive, fast-growing, with early invasion of the skull base and orbit, and has a poor prognosis", "It is usually cured with surgery alone", "It only occurs in young children"]'::jsonb, 2, 'SNUC is a highly aggressive, rapidly enlarging, high-grade malignancy of the sinonasal tract. It presents with advanced local destruction and requires multimodality therapy (chemotherapy, radiation, and sometimes surgery), but survival rates remain low.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old woman presents with unilateral nasal obstruction and epistaxis. Endoscopy reveals a black, polypoid mass in the nasal cavity. Biopsy confirms Mucosal Melanoma. How does the staging and prognosis of mucosal melanoma of the head and neck differ from cutaneous melanoma?', '["Mucosal melanoma has a much better prognosis", "Mucosal melanoma staging starts at T3 (there are no T1 or T2 categories) because it is inherently aggressive and usually presents late", "Mucosal melanoma is highly responsive to radiation therapy", "Breslow depth is the sole determinant of survival", "It rarely metastasizes to the lungs"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Because of its aggressive nature, tendency for early hematogenous spread, and presentation at advanced stages due to the hidden anatomic location, the AJCC staging for mucosal melanoma of the head and neck omits T1 and T2 classifications entirely; all tumors are at least T3.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the primary, initial treatment modality of choice for resectable mucosal melanoma of the sinonasal tract?', '["Radiation therapy alone", "Systemic chemotherapy alone", "Complete surgical resection with negative margins", "Observation", "Topical immunotherapy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Surgery is the primary treatment for resectable mucosal melanoma. Postoperative radiation is often used to improve local control, but surgery offers the best chance for cure, although overall survival remains poor due to distant metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a large ethmoid sinus tumor undergoes a craniofacial resection. During the procedure, the cribriform plate is removed. What permanent sensory deficit is an expected consequence of this necessary surgical step?', '["Blindness", "Deafness", "Anosmia (loss of smell)", "Loss of taste (ageusia)", "Facial numbness"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The olfactory nerve fibers pass through the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone. Removal of this structure for oncologic clearance inevitably results in permanent anosmia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is a common reconstructive technique used to repair the anterior skull base defect (the "hole" between the nasal cavity and the brain) after a craniofacial resection for a sinonasal tumor?', '["A free fibula flap", "A pedicled pericranial flap (using the tissue overlying the frontal bone)", "A split-thickness skin graft", "A silicone plate", "Leaving it open to heal by secondary intention"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A vascularized pericranial flap, harvested from the forehead/scalp during the craniotomy approach, is the workhorse for achieving a watertight seal of the anterior skull base, preventing cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) leaks and meningitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following clinical findings in a patient with a parotid mass is the strongest indicator that the mass is malignant rather than benign?', '["Pain", "Large size (>4 cm)", "Ipsilateral facial nerve weakness or paralysis", "Cystic consistency", "Slow growth over several years"]'::jsonb, 2, 'While some malignant tumors can be painless and slow-growing, the development of a spontaneous facial nerve palsy in the setting of a parotid mass is pathognomonic for a malignancy invading the nerve. Benign tumors simply push the nerve aside.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In the setting of a malignant parotid tumor that has directly invaded and paralyzed the facial nerve, what is the generally accepted oncologic surgical management of the nerve?', '["Careful peeling of the tumor off the nerve to preserve function", "Sacrifice (resection) of the involved segment of the facial nerve along with the tumor to achieve clear margins", "Radiation therapy instead of surgery", "Injection of botulinum toxin into the nerve", "Wait and see if the paralysis resolves"]'::jsonb, 1, 'If the facial nerve is clinically paralyzed or heavily infiltrated by the tumor preoperatively, the involved segment must be sacrificed to ensure adequate oncologic clearance. The nerve can often be grafted simultaneously (e.g., with a great auricular nerve or sural nerve graft).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the most common malignancy of the submandibular gland?', '["Squamous cell carcinoma", "Adenoid cystic carcinoma", "Mucoepidermoid carcinoma", "Acinic cell carcinoma", "Lymphoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'While mucoepidermoid carcinoma is the most common malignancy in the parotid, adenoid cystic carcinoma is the most common malignancy in both the submandibular gland and the minor salivary glands.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 60-year-old male with a history of treated squamous cell carcinoma of the skin on his right temple 2 years ago now presents with a new, firm, 2-cm mass in the right parotid gland. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["A new primary mucoepidermoid carcinoma", "Metastatic cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma to the intraparotid lymph nodes", "A benign pleomorphic adenoma", "Warthin tumor", "Lipoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The parotid gland harbors numerous lymph nodes that serve as the primary lymphatic drainage basin for the skin of the scalp, temple, forehead, and ear. A new parotid mass in a patient with a history of cutaneous SCC in these areas must be presumed to be a metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following head and neck subsites has the HIGHEST incidence of bilateral cervical lymph node metastasis at the time of presentation?', '["True vocal cords (glottis)", "Hard palate", "Anterior two-thirds of the tongue", "Base of tongue / Supraglottic larynx", "Maxillary sinus"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Midline structures with rich, crossing lymphatic networks, particularly the base of the tongue, soft palate, nasopharynx, and supraglottic larynx, have a very high propensity for bilateral nodal metastases, necessitating bilateral neck treatment.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-year-old heavy smoker presents with a 3-cm hard mass in the right neck (Level II). Complete head and neck examination, including endoscopy, reveals no obvious primary tumor. A PET-CT scan is obtained. What is the most appropriate next diagnostic step?', '["Open neck biopsy", "Fine needle aspiration (FNA) of the neck mass", "Empiric radiation therapy", "Radical neck dissection", "Chemotherapy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In the evaluation of a neck mass suspected to be malignant, FNA is the gold standard initial diagnostic test. An open neck biopsy is contraindicated as it disrupts the fascial planes, potentially spreading the tumor and compromising definitive surgical treatment.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Following an FNA confirming squamous cell carcinoma in a neck node, the primary tumor remains occult. The patient undergoes a panendoscopy under general anesthesia. Which surgical procedure is frequently performed concurrently to help find the hidden primary?', '["Bilateral superficial parotidectomy", "Bilateral palatine tonsillectomy", "Total thyroidectomy", "Tracheostomy", "Adenoidectomy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Small, occult SCCs frequently hide deep within the crypts of the palatine tonsils. Bilateral tonsillectomy is a standard part of the diagnostic workup for a Carcinoma of Unknown Primary to evaluate for these hidden tumors.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which neck dissection procedure removes the cervical lymph nodes from levels I through V but PRESERVES the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI), the internal jugular vein (IJV), and the sternocleidomastoid muscle (SCM)?', '["Radical neck dissection", "Modified radical neck dissection (Type I)", "Modified radical neck dissection (Type III) / Functional neck dissection", "Selective neck dissection", "Extended neck dissection"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A Modified Radical Neck Dissection Type III preserves all three major non-lymphatic structures (CN XI, IJV, SCM) while removing the same lymph node levels (I-V) as a radical neck dissection. This significantly reduces postoperative shoulder morbidity.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the most debilitating long-term complication of sacrificing the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) during a classic radical neck dissection?', '["Facial droop", "Inability to swallow", "Severe shoulder drop, pain, and inability to abduct the arm above 90 degrees", "Loss of sensation to the lower neck", "Hoarseness"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The spinal accessory nerve innervates the trapezius muscle. Its loss causes "shoulder syndrome" (trapezius palsy), leading to severe, chronic shoulder pain, drooping, and loss of abduction strength.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During a neck dissection involving Level IIb (submuscular recess), which cranial nerve is at highest risk of injury due to its location traversing this specific area?', '["Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)", "Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI)", "Vagus nerve (CN X)", "Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)", "Facial nerve (CN VII)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The spinal accessory nerve classically divides Level II into IIa (anterior/inferior to the nerve) and IIb (posterior/superior to the nerve). Dissection in Level IIb puts CN XI at significant risk.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Injury to the marginal mandibular branch of the facial nerve during a submandibular gland excision or Level I neck dissection results in what clinical deficit?', '["Inability to close the eye tightly", "Inability to wrinkle the forehead", "Asymmetry of the lower lip during smiling or showing teeth", "Numbness of the chin", "Deviation of the tongue"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The marginal mandibular branch innervates the depressor anguli oris. Injury causes the affected side of the lower lip to remain elevated when the patient smiles or grimaces, pulling the lip asymmetrically toward the normal side.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The chorda tympani nerve is a branch of the facial nerve that joins the lingual nerve in the infratemporal fossa. What two specific functions does the chorda tympani provide?', '["Motor to the tongue and touch sensation", "Taste to the anterior 2/3 of the tongue and parasympathetic secretomotor fibers to the submandibular/sublingual glands", "Motor to the masseter and pain sensation to the teeth", "Hearing and balance", "Taste to the posterior 1/3 of the tongue and parotid secretion"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The chorda tympani carries special visceral afferent (taste) fibers for the anterior two-thirds of the tongue and general visceral efferent (parasympathetic) fibers to the submandibular ganglion.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient undergoing a total thyroidectomy for a massive goiter develops postoperative stridor and respiratory distress in the recovery room. The neck dressing is tight and the neck is visibly swollen. What is the most critical and immediate next step?', '["Administer intravenous calcium", "Order a stat CT scan of the neck", "Immediately open the surgical incision at the bedside to evacuate the hematoma", "Perform a tracheostomy", "Administer high-dose steroids"]'::jsonb, 2, 'An expanding hematoma in the tight anterior neck compartment can cause rapid, fatal airway compression and venous engorgement of the larynx. The incision must be opened immediately to decompress the airway before transferring the patient back to the OR.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the most common cause of unilateral vocal cord paralysis in an adult?', '["Viral neuropathy", "Idiopathic", "Malignancy (lung, thyroid, or esophageal cancer) invading the recurrent laryngeal nerve", "Surgical trauma (e.g., thyroidectomy, anterior cervical discectomy)", "Stroke"]'::jsonb, 3, 'While malignancies are a major cause, iatrogenic surgical trauma (specifically thyroid, parathyroid, carotid, or anterior cervical spine surgery) is the single most common identified cause of unilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve paralysis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient has a known left vocal cord paralysis from a prior thoracic surgery. They complain of a weak, breathy voice and occasional choking on liquids. What is the pathophysiological reason for these symptoms?', '["The airway is completely obstructed", "The paralyzed vocal cord cannot adduct (move to the midline) to meet the functioning cord, resulting in a glottic gap during phonation and swallowing", "The tongue is weak", "The esophagus is strictured", "The vocal cords are hyperactive"]'::jsonb, 1, 'When a vocal cord is paralyzed in a paramedian or lateral position, it cannot close completely against the normal cord. This gap allows air to escape during speech (breathy voice) and allows liquids to penetrate the airway during swallowing (aspiration).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is a common, minimally invasive surgical procedure performed to improve voice and prevent aspiration in a patient with a symptomatic unilateral vocal cord paralysis?', '["Total laryngectomy", "Tracheostomy", "Vocal cord injection (injection laryngoplasty) to bulk up and medialize the paralyzed cord", "Partial glossectomy", "Radiation therapy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Injection laryngoplasty involves injecting a filler material (e.g., hyaluronic acid, calcium hydroxylapatite, or autologous fat) into the paralyzed vocal cord, pushing it toward the midline so the functioning cord can close against it.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which laryngeal muscle is the ONLY muscle responsible for opening (abducting) the true vocal cords, and which nerve innervates it?', '["Lateral cricoarytenoid; Recurrent laryngeal nerve", "Thyroarytenoid; Superior laryngeal nerve", "Cricothyroid; Superior laryngeal nerve", "Posterior cricoarytenoid; Recurrent laryngeal nerve", "Interarytenoid; Recurrent laryngeal nerve"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The posterior cricoarytenoid muscle is the sole abductor of the vocal folds. Like all intrinsic laryngeal muscles (except the cricothyroid), it is innervated by the recurrent laryngeal nerve.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve innervates the cricothyroid muscle. What is the primary function of the cricothyroid muscle on the vocal cords?', '["It opens (abducts) them", "It closes (adducts) them", "It shortens and relaxes them", "It elongates and tenses them, raising the pitch of the voice", "It has no function on the vocal cords"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The cricothyroid muscle tilts the thyroid cartilage anteriorly, which stretches, tenses, and thins the vocal cords. This action is primarily responsible for raising the pitch of the voice (e.g., shouting or singing high notes).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A singer undergoes a minor thyroid procedure. Postoperatively, she has a normal speaking voice but complains of an inability to reach high notes and a weak projection when shouting. Both vocal cords move normally. Which nerve was likely injured?', '["Recurrent laryngeal nerve", "Hypoglossal nerve", "Internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve", "External branch of the superior laryngeal nerve", "Phrenic nerve"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Injury to the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve paralyzes the cricothyroid muscle. Because the recurrent laryngeal nerves are intact, normal speech and airway are preserved, but the loss of vocal cord tension prevents the production of high-pitched sounds.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old male with a history of radiation therapy for Hodgkin''s lymphoma 20 years ago presents with a firm, 2-cm nodule in his thyroid gland. FNA reveals "Orphan Annie eye" nuclei and psammoma bodies. What is the diagnosis?', '["Follicular thyroid carcinoma", "Medullary thyroid carcinoma", "Papillary thyroid carcinoma", "Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma", "Hashimoto''s thyroiditis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Prior neck radiation is a major risk factor for Papillary Thyroid Carcinoma. The cytologic features of optically clear nuclei ("Orphan Annie eyes"), nuclear grooves, and psammoma bodies are pathognomonic for PTC.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is the most important prognostic factor determining survival in patients with well-differentiated (papillary and follicular) thyroid cancer?', '["Tumor size", "Patient age at the time of diagnosis", "Presence of lymph node metastases", "Gender", "Multifocality"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Age is uniquely the most critical prognostic factor in well-differentiated thyroid cancer. The AJCC staging system is heavily age-dependent (the cutoff historically was 45, currently 55 years old), where younger patients rarely die from the disease even with distant metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old female is diagnosed with Papillary Thyroid Carcinoma based on an FNA of a 1.5 cm nodule. An ultrasound of the neck shows no suspicious lymph nodes. What is the standard, accepted initial surgical management for this patient?', '["Observation", "Radioactive iodine ablation only", "Thyroid lobectomy (isthmusectomy) OR total thyroidectomy, depending on patient/surgeon preference and risk factors", "Total thyroidectomy and bilateral radical neck dissection", "Chemotherapy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'For small, unifocal PTC without clinically positive nodes (T1bN0 or T2N0), recent guidelines support either a lobectomy or a total thyroidectomy. Lobectomy avoids the risks of bilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve injury and permanent hypoparathyroidism.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why are prophylactic (elective) neck dissections generally NOT recommended for the lateral neck in patients with Papillary Thyroid Carcinoma?', '["Because PTC does not spread to lymph nodes", "Because nodal metastases are completely eradicated by radioactive iodine", "Because while lateral node metastases are common, they do not significantly impact overall survival, and removing them prophylactically adds unnecessary morbidity", "Because the lateral neck nodes are inaccessible", "Because chemotherapy is more effective"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Microscopic nodal metastasis in PTC is extremely common (up to 50%), but survival in well-differentiated thyroid cancer is so high that elective lateral neck dissection does not improve overall survival. Therapeutic neck dissection is performed only for clinically evident disease.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 40-year-old woman undergoes an FNA of a 3-cm thyroid nodule. The cytology result is "Follicular Neoplasm" (Bethesda Category IV). Why is this result considered "indeterminate," preventing a definitive diagnosis of malignancy?', '["The cells are too small to see", "FNA cannot evaluate for capsular or vascular invasion, which is the sole histologic criteria distinguishing a benign follicular adenoma from a malignant follicular carcinoma", "FNA needles cannot penetrate follicular tumors", "The staining is usually incorrect", "Follicular cells look identical to lymphocytes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A true follicular carcinoma is defined by its architectural invasion into the tumor capsule or adjacent blood vessels. FNA only provides isolated cells and cannot show this architecture. A diagnostic surgical lobectomy is required for final diagnosis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does the typical pattern of metastasis for Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma differ from that of Papillary Thyroid Carcinoma?', '["Follicular carcinoma never metastasizes", "Follicular carcinoma spreads primarily via the lymphatics to cervical nodes, while papillary spreads hematogenously", "Follicular carcinoma has a higher propensity for hematogenous (bloodborne) spread to distant sites like the bone and lungs, while papillary primarily spreads to local lymph nodes", "Both spread exclusively to the brain", "Both spread equally"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Because follicular carcinoma has a tendency for vascular invasion, it is more likely to present with distant hematogenous metastases (especially osteolytic bone lesions) compared to the lymphotropic papillary carcinoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the primary role of Radioactive Iodine (I-131) ablation in the management of well-differentiated thyroid cancer?', '["To treat the primary tumor instead of surgery", "To destroy any normal remnant thyroid tissue after a total thyroidectomy, facilitating the use of thyroglobulin as a tumor marker, and to treat microscopic metastatic disease", "To shrink the tumor before surgery", "To treat medullary thyroid cancer", "To treat anaplastic thyroid cancer"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Postoperative RAI ablation is used in high-risk well-differentiated thyroid cancer to ablate residual thyroid tissue. This ensures that any future rise in serum thyroglobulin (Tg) definitively indicates tumor recurrence.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why is Radioactive Iodine (I-131) completely ineffective in the treatment of Medullary Thyroid Carcinoma?', '["The tumor cells are resistant to radiation", "Medullary carcinoma arises from parafollicular C-cells, which do not concentrate or metabolize iodine", "The iodine causes the tumor to grow faster", "Medullary carcinoma lacks a blood supply", "It is only effective in children"]'::jsonb, 1, 'RAI relies on the iodine-concentrating ability of thyroid follicular cells. Because medullary carcinoma originates from neuroendocrine C-cells, it has no NIS (sodium-iodide symporter) and cannot take up RAI.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 30-year-old man with a family history of Medullary Thyroid Carcinoma and pheochromocytoma (MEN 2A) is found to have a RET proto-oncogene mutation. What is the recommended management for his thyroid gland?', '["Wait for a nodule to appear and perform an FNA", "Annual ultrasound screening", "Prophylactic total thyroidectomy", "Administration of propylthiouracil (PTU)", "Radioactive iodine ablation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Because penetrance of medullary thyroid carcinoma in MEN 2 is nearly 100%, prophylactic total thyroidectomy is recommended, often in early childhood depending on the specific RET mutation codon.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which biochemical tumor marker is highly sensitive and specific for monitoring disease recurrence in a patient who has been treated for Medullary Thyroid Carcinoma?', '["Thyroglobulin", "Carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) and Serum Calcitonin", "Parathyroid hormone (PTH)", "Thyroid stimulating hormone (TSH)", "Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Calcitonin is the primary secretory product of the C-cells and serves as the most sensitive tumor marker for medullary thyroid cancer. CEA is also frequently elevated and tracked.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-year-old woman presents with a rapidly expanding, rock-hard anterior neck mass over the past month. She complains of progressive stridor, dysphagia, and hoarseness. FNA reveals bizarre, pleomorphic spindle and giant cells with frequent mitoses. What is the diagnosis?', '["Papillary thyroid carcinoma", "Riedel''s thyroiditis", "Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma", "Medullary thyroid carcinoma", "Thyroid lymphoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma is one of the most aggressive and lethal malignancies in humans. It typically occurs in the elderly, presents as a rapidly growing, fixed mass causing airway compromise, and has a dismal prognosis (survival often measured in months).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following describes the most common clinical presentation of a Parathyroid Adenoma?', '["A large, painful neck mass", "Hypercalcemia discovered incidentally on routine routine blood work, often accompanied by fatigue, bone pain, and kidney stones", "Severe hypocalcemia causing tetany (Chvostek''s sign)", "Hoarseness and stridor", "Rapid weight loss and palpitations"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Primary hyperparathyroidism, most commonly caused by a single benign parathyroid adenoma, often presents subtly ("stones, bones, abdominal groans, and psychiatric overtones") or is discovered entirely asymptomatically on a chemistry panel showing high serum calcium.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient is diagnosed with primary hyperparathyroidism. A Sestamibi scan localizes an adenoma posterior to the right lower thyroid pole. During the minimally invasive parathyroidectomy, the surgeon utilizes intraoperative rapid PTH monitoring. What result confirms that the hyperfunctioning adenoma has been successfully removed?', '["The PTH level rises by 50%", "The PTH level drops by >50% from the highest pre-excision baseline within 10-15 minutes of removing the gland", "The PTH level remains unchanged", "The serum calcium level drops to normal within 2 minutes", "The PTH level drops to absolute zero"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Because intact PTH has a very short half-life (3-5 minutes), the Miami criterion states that a drop in intraoperative PTH of at least 50% at 10 minutes post-excision reliably indicates that all hyperfunctioning parathyroid tissue has been removed.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old patient presents with primary hyperparathyroidism. Neck exploration reveals that all four parathyroid glands are enlarged. What is the diagnosis, and what underlying genetic syndrome must be suspected?', '["Parathyroid carcinoma; MEN 1", "Four-gland parathyroid hyperplasia; Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia type 1 (MEN 1) or MEN 2A", "Single parathyroid adenoma; Gorlin syndrome", "Secondary hyperparathyroidism; Chronic kidney disease", "Tertiary hyperparathyroidism; Vitamin D deficiency"]'::jsonb, 1, 'When all four glands are enlarged, it is parathyroid hyperplasia rather than an adenoma. This is highly associated with familial syndromes like MEN 1 (3 Ps: Parathyroid, Pituitary, Pancreas) and MEN 2A.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the standard surgical treatment for four-gland parathyroid hyperplasia?', '["Removal of one gland", "Subtotal parathyroidectomy (removal of 3.5 glands, leaving a 50 mg remnant) OR total parathyroidectomy with autotransplantation of a small piece of tissue into the forearm or SCM muscle", "Total parathyroidectomy without autotransplantation", "No surgery, only medical management", "Radiation therapy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The goal is to remove the excess functioning tissue while leaving just enough behind to prevent permanent, severe hypocalcemia. Autotransplantation into the forearm allows for easy re-operation under local anesthesia if the patient becomes hypercalcemic again.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient undergoing a total thyroidectomy for anaplastic thyroid carcinoma has all four parathyroid glands inadvertently removed or devascularized. What classic clinical sign of hypocalcemia might they develop in the recovery room?', '["Trousseau''s sign (carpopedal spasm when a blood pressure cuff is inflated above systolic pressure)", "Tinel''s sign", "Homan''s sign", "Battle''s sign", "Cullen''s sign"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Trousseau''s sign and Chvostek''s sign (facial twitching when tapping the facial nerve) are classic physical exam findings indicating neuromuscular irritability caused by severe hypocalcemia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old woman presents with a slow-growing, pulsatile mass in the lateral neck just below the angle of the jaw. The mass can be moved horizontally but is restricted from moving vertically (Fontaine''s sign). What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Branchial cleft cyst", "Carotid body tumor (paraganglioma)", "Aneurysm of the external carotid artery", "Cervical lymphadenitis", "Schwannoma of the vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A carotid body tumor is a highly vascular paraganglioma arising at the carotid bifurcation. Fontaine''s sign describes its mobility: it is tethered to the carotid artery, preventing vertical movement, but can be shifted side-to-side.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'An MRI or CT angiogram of a Carotid Body Tumor will classically demonstrate which radiologic sign?', '["The \"soap bubble\" sign", "The \"dumbbell\" sign", "The \"lyre\" sign (splaying or widening of the angle between the internal and external carotid arteries by a highly vascular mass)", "The \"rat tail\" sign", "The \"thumbprint\" sign"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Because the tumor arises from the chemoreceptor cells situated exactly at the bifurcation of the common carotid artery, its growth pushes the internal and external carotid arteries apart, creating the classic "lyre" sign.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Prior to surgical resection of a functional secreting paraganglioma, what critical preoperative medical management must be instituted to prevent a life-threatening intraoperative crisis?', '["Administration of high-dose corticosteroids", "Preoperative radiation", "Alpha-adrenergic blockade (e.g., phenoxybenzamine) followed by beta-blockade", "Systemic chemotherapy", "Strict fluid restriction"]'::jsonb, 2, 'If a paraganglioma secretes catecholamines (like a pheochromocytoma), manipulating the tumor during surgery can cause a massive release, leading to hypertensive crisis, arrhythmias, and stroke. Alpha-blockade is mandatory to normalize blood pressure before surgery.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old female presents with pulsatile tinnitus and progressive right-sided hearing loss. Otoscopy reveals a reddish, pulsatile mass rising from the floor of the middle ear behind the tympanic membrane. What is the diagnosis?', '["Cholesteatoma", "Glomus tympanicum or Glomus jugulare (paraganglioma)", "Otitis media", "Acoustic neuroma", "Meniere''s disease"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A glomus tumor (paraganglioma) in the middle ear classically presents with pulsatile tinnitus. If it is confined to the promontory, it is a glomus tympanicum; if it arises from the jugular bulb and extends into the ear, it is a glomus jugulare.')
